939453125, 0.024627685546875, -0.033966064453125, -0.01097869873046875, 0.0174102783203125, 0.0064239501953125, 0.031890869140625, -0.01036834716796875, -0.00958251953125, 0.0071563720703125, -0.003421783447265625, -0.0183563232421875, -0.00039196014404296875, 0.0015077590942382812, 0.023895263671875, 0.003047943115234375, -0.0251312255859375, 0.002399444580078125, 0.005161285400390625, -0.0164794921875, 0.0197906494140625, 0.018585205078125, -0.002777099609375, 0.022125244140625, -0.003452301025390625, 0.036376953125, -0.01523590087890625, -0.01154327392578125, 0.00162506103515625, 0.01117706298828125, -0.022857666015625, 0.0035686492919921875, 0.023345947265625, -0.032867431640625, 0.00217437744140625, -0.0245361328125, -0.015350341796875, 0.00554656982421875, 0.01416015625, 0.00890350341796875, -0.054443359375, 0.03411865234375, -0.02081298828125, -0.01023101806640625, -0.005687713623046875, 0.0062713623046875, -0.01113128662109375, -0.006183624267578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**1. Introduction and Definitions**\n\nWe study the relationship between weak convergence of synaptic input measures and the firing behavior of sparse threshold-reset networks in the fast-synapse limit. Throughout, we clarify the following definitions and model assumptions:\n\n- *Componentwise weak convergence*: Let $(\\mu_N^+, \\mu_N^-)$ be a sequence of finite signed Borel measures on $\\mathbb{R}$ (representing excitatory and inhibitory synaptic input times and strengths). We say $(\\mu_N^+, \\mu_N^-)$ converges componentwise weakly to $(\\mu^+, \\mu^-)$ if, for every bounded continuous function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$,\n  \\[\n  \\int f\\,d\\mu_N^\\pm \\to \\int f\\,d\\mu^\\pm \\quad \\text{as } N\\to\\infty.\n  \\]\n- *Fast-synapse limit*: The regime where all synaptic kernel delays $\\varepsilon_N$ tend to zero, i.e., $\\varepsilon_N\\downarrow 0$ as $N\\to\\infty$.\n- *Causal event protocol*: Spikes are processed in strict temporal order. After a spike, the membrane potential is clamped at reset value for a fixed refractory period $\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}>0$. Synaptic kernels $K_\\varepsilon$ are smooth ($C^\\infty$), nonnegative, compactly supported on $[0,\\varepsilon]$, and satisfy $\\int K_\\varepsilon = 1$. All synaptic delays are strictly positive.\n- *Typewise-mixing*: Let $G_N$ be a random directed graph on $N$ vertices, partitioned into finitely many synaptic classes. For each class, the induced subgraph is such that, for any fixed neuron, the empirical distribution of incoming edges from that class converges in total variation to the class distribution as $N\\to\\infty$.\n\n**2. Model Specification and Spike Train Construction**\n\nWe consider a leaky integrate-and-fire neuron with membrane potential $V(t)$ governed by\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt} = -\\lambda (V(t) - V_{\\mathrm{rest}}) + I_{\\mathrm{syn}}(t),\n\\]\nwhere $\\lambda\\geq 0$ is the leak rate, $V_{\\mathrm{rest}}$ is the resting potential, and $I_{\\mathrm{syn}}(t)$ is the total synaptic input. When $V(t)$ reaches threshold $\\theta$, the neuron emits a spike, $V$ is reset to $V_{\\mathrm{reset}}$, and held at $V_{\\mathrm{reset}}$ for $\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}$ (refractory period).\n\nWe construct two families of input spike trains for a target neuron, parameterized by a small delay $\\varepsilon>0$ and pulse masses $a>0$ (excitatory), $b>0$ (inhibitory):\n\n- *Excitatory-first*: The neuron receives an excitatory spike at $t=0$ and an inhibitory spike at $t=\\varepsilon$.\n- *Inhibitory-first*: The neuron receives an inhibitory spike at $t=0$ and an excitatory spike at $t=\\varepsilon$.\n\nEach spike is modeled as $a K_\\varepsilon(t)$ (excitatory) or $-b K_\\varepsilon(t-\\delta)$ (inhibitory, with $\\delta=0$ or $\\varepsilon$). As $\\varepsilon\\downarrow 0$, both input measures converge weakly to $a\\delta_0$ and $-b\\delta_0$.\n\n**3. Main Result: Firing Behavior and Robustness**\n\n*Theorem 1 (Order Sensitivity in the Fast-Synapse Limit).*  \nLet the neuron have initial state $V(0)=x$ and threshold $\\theta$. In the fast-synapse limit $\\varepsilon\\downarrow 0$, under the above protocol and model, the neuron fires in the excitatory-first case but not in the inhibitory-first case if and only if $x+a-b<\\theta\\le x+a$.\n\n*Robustness*: This firing outcome persists under perturbations of the initial state $x$ by at most $\\eta>0$, pulse masses $a,b$ by at most $\\eta$, and bounded drift $|dV/dt|\\le \\eta$, provided $\\theta$ is at least $2\\eta$ away from the endpoints $x+a-b$ and $x+a$. That is, for all $|\\delta x|, |\\delta a|, |\\delta b| \\le \\eta$ and $|dV/dt|\\le \\eta$, the firing outcome is unchanged.\n\n*Proof Outline*: After both pulses, the membrane potential is $x+a-b$ in both cases. In the excitatory-first case, $V$ may cross threshold before inhibition arrives if $x+a\\geq\\theta>x+a-b$. In the inhibitory-first case, inhibition arrives first, so $V$ never crosses threshold. The robustness follows by continuity: if $\\theta$ is at least $2\\eta$ away from the endpoints, small perturbations cannot change the firing outcome.\n\n**4. Network Construction and Propagation to Sparse Networks**\n\nLet $G_N$ be a random directed block graph with $N$ neurons, each with $q_N$ outgoing edges ($q_N\\to\\infty$, $q_N/N\\to 0$), partitioned into finitely many synaptic classes. Each neuron is either purely excitatory or inhibitory (*Dale compatibility*). The network is initialized identically in both the excitatory-first and inhibitory-first protocols, differing only in the order of paired spikes as above.\n\n*Propagation Mechanism*: For each neuron, the probability that the excitatory spike arrives before the inhibitory one is $1/2$ in the limit, due to random edge assignments and independent delays. Thus, the expected difference in firing counts between the two protocols is $1/2$ per neuron. More precisely, letting $F_N^{(E)}$ and $F_N^{(I)}$ be the total firing counts in the excitatory-first and inhibitory-first systems,\n\\[\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\frac{1}{N}(F_N^{(E)} - F_N^{(I)})\\right] \\to \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{N}(F_N^{(E)} - F_N^{(I)}) = \\frac{1}{2} + o_{L^1}(1)\n\\]\nwhere $o_{L^1}(1)$ denotes convergence to zero in $L^1$ as $N\\to\\infty$. This macroscopic difference persists after reset, as can be shown by constructing a bounded-degree subnetwork and probing at later times.\n\n**5. Mean-Field Regime and Convergence Rates**\n\nFor fixed positive-delay kernels and finitely many synaptic classes, consider directed Erd\u0151s-R\u00e9nyi graphs with mean degree $\\lambda_N\\to\\infty$, $\\lambda_N/N\\to 0$, and empirical class distribution $\\pi_N$ converging to $\\pi$. In the *typewise-mixing* regime, before any neuron\u2019s threshold is grazed, the network dynamics converge to a delayed class mean-field system:\n\n*Theorem 2 (Mean-Field Convergence).*  \nLet $X_N(t)$ denote the empirical measure of neuron states at time $t$. Then, for $t$ before the first threshold crossing,\n\\[\n\\|X_N(t) - \\bar{X}(t)\\| \\le O_P(\\lambda_N^{-1/2} + \\|\\pi_N - \\pi\\|_1)\n\\]\nwhere $\\bar{X}(t)$ is the solution to the class-averaged mean-field equation, and $O_P$ denotes stochastic boundedness in probability.\n\n*Proof Outline*: The result follows from concentration inequalities for sparse random graphs and standard mean-field arguments, using the mixing property to control deviations.\n\n**6. Discussion and Future Directions**\n\nOur results delineate two regimes: (1) In the stable averaging regime (fixed delay, typewise-mixing), macroscopic behavior is determined by class-averaged inputs and is insensitive to microscopic timing. (2) In the singular collapse regime (fast-synapse limit), componentwise weak convergence fails to capture the signed arrival-order information required by the threshold-reset mechanism, leading to macroscopic discrepancies.\n\n*Limitations and Future Work*: Weak convergence alone is insufficient for predicting network responses in the fast-synapse limit. A promising direction is to consider *ordered measure convergence*, such as convergence in the Skorokhod $J_1$ topology for cumulative input processes (see Billingsley [1, Ch. 3]), or to track the joint law of input arrival times. Formalizing such notions may yield convergence criteria that retain arrival-order information and better predict network-level computations. Explicit counterexamples, as in Section 3, demonstrate the failure of weak convergence to capture firing order effects.\n\n**Acknowledgments and References**\n\nFor formal definitions and proofs, see [1] (for weak convergence and Skorokhod topology), [2] (mean-field limits in sparse graphs), and [3] (threshold-reset dynamics and integrate-and-fire models).  \n[1] Billingsley, P. (1999). *Convergence of Probability Measures*.  \n[2] Delarue, F., Inglis, J., Rubenthaler, S., & Tanr\u00e9, E. (2015). *Global solvability of a networked integrate-and-fire model of McKean\u2013Vlasov type*. Ann. Appl. Probab.  \n[3] Gerstner,"}}